\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Physical-metabolic decoupling uncovers an evolutionary trade-off between surface adhesion and metabolic specialization.} Scatter plot of metabolic shift magnitude ($\|\mathbf  {\Theta }^{(c)}\| / \|\mathbf  {\Theta }^{(s)}\|$) versus physical adhesion affinity ($k_{\text  {attach}} / k_{\text  {shed}}$) across all 50 gut bacterial strains. Points indicate ensemble means with 2D error bars representing $\pm 1\,\text  {SEM}$ across $N=10$ random initializations. Strains are color-coded by taxonomic phylum; the blue dashed line indicates the linear regression fit with shaded 95\% confidence interval. Strains face an evolutionary trade-off: strong physical stickers persist via mechanical retention without metabolic shifts, whereas weak stickers must undergo substantial metabolic reprogramming to colonize the mucosal niche ($r = -0.371,\ p = 8.07 \times 10^{-3}$; Spearman $\rho = -0.387$).}}{6}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pollard_tradeoff}{{4}{6}{\textbf {Physical-metabolic decoupling uncovers an evolutionary trade-off between surface adhesion and metabolic specialization.} Scatter plot of metabolic shift magnitude ($\|\mathbf {\Theta }^{(c)}\| / \|\mathbf {\Theta }^{(s)}\|$) versus physical adhesion affinity ($k_{\text {attach}} / k_{\text {shed}}$) across all 50 gut bacterial strains. Points indicate ensemble means with 2D error bars representing $\pm 1\,\text {SEM}$ across $N=10$ random initializations. Strains are color-coded by taxonomic phylum; the blue dashed line indicates the linear regression fit with shaded 95\% confidence interval. Strains face an evolutionary trade-off: strong physical stickers persist via mechanical retention without metabolic shifts, whereas weak stickers must undergo substantial metabolic reprogramming to colonize the mucosal niche ($r = -0.371,\ p = 8.07 \times 10^{-3}$; Spearman $\rho = -0.387$)}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Unsupervised latent chemical axes and localized competitive pressure predict host-specific intervention divergence.} (A) Learned resource capacities ($R_0$) in a 3-dimensional latent space autonomously recover the experimental chemical perturbations: Resource 0 captures baseline syngas, Resource 1 aligns with the Methanol (MeOH) perturbation axis, and Resource 2 aligns with the Trimethylamine (TMA) perturbation axis. (B) Metabolic niche map showing strain affinities for MeOH ($\Theta _{i, \text  {MeOH}}$) versus TMA ($\Theta _{i, \text  {TMA}}$). Points are sized and colored by baseline abundance in Cow BRRS, highlighting the keystone methanogen *Methanomicrobium sp.* (C) Host inoculum bias (BRRS $-$ OSU) versus affinity asymmetry ($\Theta _{i, \text  {MeOH}} - \Theta _{i, \text  {TMA}}$), demonstrating that *Methanomicrobium sp.* occupies a region of severe host inoculum divergence. (D) Quantified localized competitive pressure on *Methanomicrobium sp.* computed using the model trained exclusively on Cow OSU. In Cow OSU, high resident competitor biomass creates an impenetrable competitive barrier (pressure increases from 5.80 at $T_1$ to 7.40 at $T_6$), suppressing the methanogen. In Cow BRRS, absence of competitors creates a niche vacuum (competitive release), allowing *Methanomicrobium sp.* to bloom.}}{7}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:german_competition}{{5}{7}{\textbf {Unsupervised latent chemical axes and localized competitive pressure predict host-specific intervention divergence.} (A) Learned resource capacities ($R_0$) in a 3-dimensional latent space autonomously recover the experimental chemical perturbations: Resource 0 captures baseline syngas, Resource 1 aligns with the Methanol (MeOH) perturbation axis, and Resource 2 aligns with the Trimethylamine (TMA) perturbation axis. (B) Metabolic niche map showing strain affinities for MeOH ($\Theta _{i, \text {MeOH}}$) versus TMA ($\Theta _{i, \text {TMA}}$). Points are sized and colored by baseline abundance in Cow BRRS, highlighting the keystone methanogen *Methanomicrobium sp.* (C) Host inoculum bias (BRRS $-$ OSU) versus affinity asymmetry ($\Theta _{i, \text {MeOH}} - \Theta _{i, \text {TMA}}$), demonstrating that *Methanomicrobium sp.* occupies a region of severe host inoculum divergence. (D) Quantified localized competitive pressure on *Methanomicrobium sp.* computed using the model trained exclusively on Cow OSU. In Cow OSU, high resident competitor biomass creates an impenetrable competitive barrier (pressure increases from 5.80 at $T_1$ to 7.40 at $T_6$), suppressing the methanogen. In Cow BRRS, absence of competitors creates a niche vacuum (competitive release), allowing *Methanomicrobium sp.* to bloom}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Cross-host transfer and prescriptive forecasting of intervention trajectories.} (A) Terminal abundance of *Methanomicrobium sp.* across passages. (B) Longitudinal dynamic passage trajectories in Cow BRRS accurately predicted by transferring the OSU-trained metabolic interaction topology, proving that low-dimensional CRMs can forecast individual-specific therapeutic and agricultural interventions.}}{8}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:german_transfer}{{6}{8}{\textbf {Cross-host transfer and prescriptive forecasting of intervention trajectories.} (A) Terminal abundance of *Methanomicrobium sp.* across passages. (B) Longitudinal dynamic passage trajectories in Cow BRRS accurately predicted by transferring the OSU-trained metabolic interaction topology, proving that low-dimensional CRMs can forecast individual-specific therapeutic and agricultural interventions}{figure.6}{}}
